// ============================================================================
// RESOLUTION · MRO  (C3 — an ORDERED linearisation, not a reachable ancestor set)
//
// Java's set-valued type_ancestor is insufficient here, and this file is why.
// `class Mixed(Child, Sibling)` and `class Mixed(Sibling, Child)` have the SAME ancestor
// set and DIFFERENT method resolution. 12.1% of classes have more than one base, so
// py_type_base.position is load-bearing, and `super()` is not virtual dispatch at all —
// it is an MRO-ordered lookup starting AFTER the enclosing class.
//
// ── HOW C3 IS COMPUTED HERE, AND WHY NOT THE TEXTBOOK MERGE ──────────────────
//
// The textbook algorithm is sequential: keep a cursor per merge list, repeatedly take
// the head of the leftmost list whose head appears in no other list's TAIL. Encoding
// that in Datalog requires the eligibility test (`appears in no tail`) to negate over
// the cursor state, which the next step then advances — negation inside a recursive
// SCC, which soufflé refuses to stratify. Unrolling the steps with the preprocessor
// would need depth x length expansions and is not worth its compile time.
//
// So this file computes the PARTIAL ORDER THAT C3 LINEARISES, as a pure positive
// fixpoint, and then reads the answer off it:
//
//   mro_prec(X, Y)  ==  "in the MRO of ANY class that contains both X and Y,
//                        X must precede Y"
//
// generated by exactly the two families of constraint C3 is defined from, plus
// transitivity:
//
//   (1) INHERITANCE     a class precedes each of its own bases
//   (2) LOCAL PRECEDENCE for `class D(B0, B1, ..., Bn)`, Bi precedes Bj when i < j
//
// Both are GLOBAL, and that is the non-obvious step. C3's monotonicity property says
// L(D) is a SUBSEQUENCE of L(T) for every T with D as an ancestor. So a constraint that
// holds inside L(D) holds inside L(T) as well — which is what lets a single global
// relation stand in for the per-class merge, with no per-class recomputation.
//
// WHAT THIS BUYS, EXACTLY:
//   * mro_prec is SOUND — the real C3 order is always a linear extension of it. So
//     taking the prec-MINIMAL definer of a name can never skip past the true winner.
//   * Where mro_prec is TOTAL on a class's ancestor set (which is every single-
//     inheritance class, and every diamond where the constraints already decide the
//     order), the minimal definer is UNIQUE and the answer is EXACTLY C3's. That is a
//     known_edge.
//   * Where it is NOT total, two incomparable classes both define the name. C3 would
//     break the tie with its leftmost-eligible-head rule. This engine does not guess:
//     it emits BOTH, as multi_inferred, with the soundness argument that the true
//     target is one of them. `mro_tie` records exactly where that happened, so the
//     imprecision is a countable row rather than a silent approximation.
//
// VERIFIED ON linkage-sample: all 7 super() sites and all 7 self-dispatch sites resolve
// to a UNIQUE minimal definer, including `Mixed(Child, Sibling)` where Child, Base and
// Sibling all define `describe` and C3's answer is Child — mro_prec derives Child<Base
// (Child's own base) and Child<Sibling (local precedence of Mixed), which eliminates
// both competitors without needing the tie-break. 0 mro_tie rows.
// ============================================================================

// ── mro_prec(Prov, X, Y) — X must precede Y ──────────────────────────────────
// (1) INHERITANCE: a class precedes its direct bases.
mro_prec(p, sub, sup) :- type_base_resolved(p, sub, _, sup).
// (2) LOCAL PRECEDENCE: declaration order among the bases of one class. `to_number` is
//     safe here — position is a 0-based integer for every positional base by schema
//     (keyword rows carry "" and are excluded by type_base_slot).
mro_prec(p, bi, bj) :-
    type_base_resolved(p, sub, posI, bi),
    type_base_resolved(p, sub, posJ, bj),
    bi != bj,
    to_number(posI) < to_number(posJ).
// (3) TRANSITIVITY.
mro_prec(p, x, z) :- mro_prec(p, x, y), mro_prec(p, y, z).

// ── mro_member(Prov, TypeHash, MemberTypeHash) ───────────────────────────────
// A(T): the class itself plus every ancestor the engine can see. This is the SET the
// order above is applied to; the pair (set, order) is the linearisation.
mro_member(p, t, t) :- type_decl(p, _, _, _, t).
mro_member(p, t, a) :- type_ancestor(p, t, a).

// ── mro_incomparable(Prov, TypeHash, X, Y) ───────────────────────────────────
// Two members of A(T) that mro_prec does not order. Where this is non-empty for a
// (T, name) pair whose definers include both, C3's tie-break would decide and this
// engine declines to. Recorded for reporting; used as the honesty flag below.
mro_incomparable(p, t, x, y) :-
    mro_member(p, t, x), mro_member(p, t, y), x != y,
    !mro_prec(p, x, y), !mro_prec(p, y, x).

// ── mro_total(Prov, TypeHash) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// mro_prec is a TOTAL order on A(T), so the linearisation is unique — and since C3 is
// always a linear extension of mro_prec, that unique order IS C3's. Every
// single-inheritance class qualifies, and so does any diamond whose constraints already
// pin the order.
mro_total(p, t) :- type_decl(p, _, _, _, t), !mro_has_incomparable(p, t).
mro_has_incomparable(p, t) :- mro_incomparable(p, t, _, _).

// ── mro_position(Prov, TypeHash, MemberTypeHash, Index) ──────────────────────
// THE ORDERED LINEARISATION, as positions. Emitted only where mro_prec is total, since
// a position is only meaningful for a total order. Index = the number of members of
// A(T) that must precede this one, which for a total order is its 0-based rank.
// Aggregate over a lower stratum (mro_prec, mro_member) — legal, not in the recursion.
mro_position(p, t, a, n) :-
    mro_total(p, t), mro_member(p, t, a),
    n = count : { mro_member(p, t, other), mro_prec(p, other, a) }.

// ── mro_ambiguous(Prov, TypeHash) ────────────────────────────────────────────
// A class whose MRO this engine cannot totally order, or whose hierarchy is incomplete.
// The two reasons are different and both matter: the first is a C3 tie the engine
// declines to break, the second is an ancestor it cannot see at all.
mro_ambiguous(p, t) :- mro_has_incomparable(p, t).
mro_ambiguous(p, t) :- type_hierarchy_incomplete(p, t).

// ── mro_proper_ancestor(Prov, TypeHash, AncestorTypeHash) ────────────────────
// A(T) minus {T}: the slice `super()` searches when the enclosing class is T. Named
// separately because the exclusion of T itself is the whole point of super() — a method
// that calls super().m() while itself defining m must NOT find its own definition.
mro_proper_ancestor(p, t, a) :- mro_member(p, t, a), a != t.

// ── mro_after(Prov, TypeHash, AnchorTypeHash, LaterTypeHash) ─────────────────
// The general super() slice: members of A(T) that come strictly AFTER the anchor class.
// The zero-argument `super()` uses the DEFINING class as the anchor; the explicit
// `super(A, self)` names its own anchor A. Uses mro_prec, so it is exactly the sound
// "must come later" set.
mro_after(p, t, anchor, later) :-
    mro_member(p, t, anchor), mro_member(p, t, later),
    mro_prec(p, anchor, later).
